    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Moved to revolutionary politics by his brother's execution in 1887, Lenin became a Marxist theorist, led the Bolshevik faction from 1903 and headed the government that took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> in October 1917.</li><li>His government redistributed land, nationalised banks and large industry, left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> at a heavy cost in territory, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>, and by 1918 had made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a> carried out the Red Terror.</li><li>Supporters see a champion of revolutionary socialism and internationalism; critics accuse him of founding a totalitarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> responsible for mass killings and repression, and historians still disagree on how far Stalin continued his work.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1917 the German government allowed 32 Russian citizens, Lenin among them, to travel through its territory to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; the train was said to be sealed, but it was not truly sealed, and the passengers were allowed to get off, for example to spend a night in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

