    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1870</span><span class="rf-text">Vladimir Ilyich Ulyanov is born in Simbirsk</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1887</span><span class="rf-text">His brother Aleksandr is executed; Lenin enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> University and is expelled after a student demonstration</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1903</span><span class="rf-text">At the second Congress of the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> the party splits into Lenin's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> and Martov's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mensheviks">Mensheviks</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1917</span><span class="rf-text">Lenin returns to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in April and in October the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> overthrow the Provisional Government; he becomes head of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-peoples-commissars">Council of People's Commissars</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1918</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> signs the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a>, the Red Terror begins, and Lenin is shot and severely wounded by Fanny Kaplan</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1921</span><span class="rf-text">Lenin introduces the New Economic Policy and bans factions within the party</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1922</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> is formed; Lenin has his first stroke in May</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1924</span><span class="rf-text">Lenin dies at Gorki and is laid in a mausoleum on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-square">Red Square</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto.jpg" alt="The Communist Manifesto" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Communist-manifesto.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Friedrich Engels, Karl Marx, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lenin was born Vladimir Ilyich Ulyanov in Simbirsk, now Ulyanovsk, on 22 April 1870, the third of eight children. His father, Ilya Nikolayevich Ulyanov, came from a family of former serfs and rose to be Director of Public Schools for the province, a post that gave him hereditary noble status; he oversaw the foundation of over 450 schools. His mother, Maria Alexandrovna, née Blank, was the well-educated daughter of a physician and largely indifferent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. His parents were monarchists and liberal conservatives, committed to the emancipation reform of 1861, and Lenin excelled at the Simbirsk Classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">Gymnasium</a>.</p><p>In January 1886 his father died of a brain haemorrhage, and Lenin, then 15, renounced his belief in God. In May 1887 his elder brother Aleksandr, who had joined a revolutionary cell bent on assassinating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Alexander III, was executed. Lenin nevertheless graduated at the top of his class with a gold medal and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> University in August 1887 to study law. He took part in a December demonstration against restrictions on student societies, was arrested and expelled, and was exiled to the family's Kokushkino estate, where he read Chernyshevsky's What Is to Be Done? In Samara from 1889 he translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">Communist Manifesto</a> and read Plekhanov, and in 1890 he took his law examinations externally at the University of St Petersburg, gaining the equivalent of a first-class degree with honours.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Vladimir Lenin&#8221; cites where it covers Childhood: 1870–1887, University and political radicalisation: 1887–1893:</p><ul><li>Yohanan Petrovsky-Shtern, Lenin's Jewish Question (Yale University Press, 2010)</li><li>Stefan T. Possony, Lenin: The Compulsive Revolutionary (Routledge, 2017)</li><li>Robert Service, Lenin: A Biography (Macmillan, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin.jpg" alt="Joseph Stalin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StalinCropped1943.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Franklin D. Roosevelt Library Public Domain Photographs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lenin moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> in late 1893 and rose to a senior position in a Marxist cell. He was arrested with about 40 activists and charged with sedition, spent a year in prison, and in February 1897 was sentenced without trial to three years' exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, at Shushenskoye, where Nadezhda Krupskaya joined him and they married on 10 July 1898. In 1900 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> to help launch the newspaper Iskra. He first used the pseudonym Lenin in December 1901, and in 1902 published What Is to Be Done?, setting out the need for a vanguard party. At the second Congress of the party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in July 1903 Lenin's supporters won a majority on the question of strong party leadership; he called them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, and Martov's followers became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mensheviks">Mensheviks</a>, who accused Lenin of being a despot and autocrat.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution-of-1905">Revolution of 1905</a> Lenin urged violent insurrection and endorsed robbing post offices, trains and banks to fund the party; the best known robbery, of the State Bank in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> in June 1907, was led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he urged socialists to turn the war into a continent-wide civil war. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917 he returned through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> in April and set out his April Theses against the Provisional Government. After the July Days he was accused of being a German agent and hid. On 10 October the Central Committee voted ten to two for an armed insurrection, against the objections of Zinoviev and Kamenev, and the Provisional Government was overthrown that month. Lenin proposed Trotsky as head of the new government but accepted the chairmanship himself.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk.jpg" alt="Treaty of Brest-Litovsk" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traktat_brzeski_1918.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The new government's first decrees nationalised the landed estates of the aristocracy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> for redistribution to the peasants, set an eight-hour working day, guaranteed free secular education and nationalised the banks. Lenin urged peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> resumed their advance in February 1918, persuaded a small majority of the Central Committee to accept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a>, signed on 3 March. It transferred to German control 26% of the former Empire's population and three-quarters of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and iron deposits. The Constituent Assembly elected in November 1917, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> won about a quarter of the vote, was forcibly disbanded in January 1918. The Left Socialist-Revolutionaries left the government in March 1918, and by 1918 and 1919 other socialists had been expelled from the soviets, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had become a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, which Trotsky organised, defeated the White armies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>, and the Soviet government also fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-soviet-war">Polish–Soviet War</a>, in which the Red Army was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-riga">Peace of Riga</a> was signed in March 1921. In March 1919 Lenin helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Communist International</a>. Facing famine, strikes and uprisings, in 1921 he introduced the New Economic Policy, which allowed peasants to sell produce on the open market and permitted small private industry, while basic industry, transport and foreign trade stayed under state control. He called it "state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>", and many Bolsheviks thought it a betrayal of socialist principles. On 30 December 1922 the Congress of Soviets ratified the formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, a plan Lenin had pressed against Stalin's proposal to absorb the republics into the Russian state.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Vladimir Lenin&#8221; cites where it covers Organising the Soviet government: 1917–1918, Social, legal, and economic reform: 1917–1918, Treaty of Brest-Litovsk: 1917–1918, Anti-Kulak campaigns, Cheka, and Red Terror: 1918–1922:</p><ul><li>Robert Service, Lenin: A Biography (Macmillan, 2000)</li><li>Louis Fischer, The Life of Lenin (Weidenfeld and Nicolson, 1964)</li><li>James Ryan, Lenin's Terror: The Ideological Origins of Early Soviet State Violence (Routledge, 2012)</li><li>James D. White, Lenin: The Practice and Theory of Revolution (Palgrave, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-kautsky"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-kautsky.jpg" alt="Karl Kautsky" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-kautsky">Karl Kautsky</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_Kautsky_High_Res.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lenin repeatedly stressed the need for terror and violence, and in December 1917 ordered the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a>. In August 1918 he telegraphed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> of Penza to suppress a peasant insurrection by publicly hanging at least 100 "known kulaks, rich men, [and] bloodsuckers". The Red Terror was begun by a decree of September 1918; no records survive to give a figure for its dead, and historians' estimates range from 10,000 to 15,000 and from 50,000 to 140,000. Lenin never witnessed the killings and publicly distanced himself from them, but he regularly called for executions in coded telegrams and confidential notes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">Concentration camps</a> were set up from 1919, holding about 70,000 prisoners by October 1923, and their inmates were used as slave labour. In May 1922 he issued a decree calling for the execution of anti-Bolshevik priests, causing between 14,000 and 20,000 deaths. The famine of 1921 killed around five million people, and requisitioning and grain exports were said to have worsened it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kronstadt-rebellion">Kronstadt rebellion</a> of March 1921 was crushed with thousands of deaths.</p><p>Opposition came from the left as well. Left Communists and the Workers' Opposition criticised the decline of democratic institutions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-kautsky">Karl Kautsky</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg">Rosa Luxemburg</a> condemned the regime's anti-democratic nature, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-kropotkin">Peter Kropotkin</a> called the seizure of power "the burial of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a>". Biographers such as Robert Service describe a man intolerant of opposition, for whom the end always justified the means.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Vladimir Lenin&#8221; cites where it covers Declining health and conflict with Stalin: 1920–1923:</p><ul><li>Louis Fischer, The Life of Lenin (Weidenfeld and Nicolson, 1964)</li><li>Robert Service, Lenin: A Biography (Macmillan, 2000)</li><li>T. H. Rigby, Lenin's Government: Sovnarkom 1917–1922 (Cambridge University Press, 1979)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/red-square"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-square.jpg" alt="Red Square" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/red-square">Red Square</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kremlin_and_Red_Square.1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">mos.ru, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lenin's health suffered after Fanny Kaplan shot him on 30 August 1918; she was captured at once and executed on 3 September. By the second half of 1921 he had hyperacusis, insomnia and regular headaches. In April 1922 surgeons removed bullets from the 1918 attempt, but the symptoms continued, and his doctors were unsure of the cause. He had his first stroke in May 1922, a second in December and a third in March 1923, which took away his speech. From December 1922 to January 1923 he dictated what became known as Lenin's Testament, recommending that Stalin be removed as General Secretary and criticising Trotsky's self-assurance. In the Georgian affair he saw Stalin's proposal to fold the Soviet republics into the Russian state as Great Russian ethnic chauvinism, and he grew increasingly critical of Stalin, who from December 1922 controlled who had access to him.</p><p>On 21 January 1924 Lenin fell into a coma and died at the age of 53. Around a million mourners viewed the body over three days at the House of Trade Unions, and his funeral on 27 January ended with his body being placed in a mausoleum on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-square">Red Square</a>. Trotsky was absent and later claimed that Stalin had sent him the wrong date. Against Krupskaya's protests, the body was embalmed for long-term public display, and in 1925 an institute was set up to dissect the brain, which showed severe sclerosis. Stalin succeeded him as the pre-eminent figure in the Soviet government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/richard-pipes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-pipes.jpg" alt="Richard Pipes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/richard-pipes">Richard Pipes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richard_Pipes_2004_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mariusz Kubik, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lenin's administration laid the framework for the system of government that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> for seven decades and provided the model for later Communist-led states. After his death Stalin's administration established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a>, and states claiming to follow Lenin's ideas formed variants such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maoism">Maoism</a> and Castroism, though many Western communists later thought his ideas irrelevant to their goals. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> a cult of personality grew up around him, which Krupskaya criticised and Lenin had not wanted; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> was renamed Leningrad in 1924, and his writings were treated like scripture and edited, with those unhelpful to Stalin left unpublished until the Russian archive was opened after 1991, when over 6,000 writings were found to have gone unpublished.</p><p>Historians disagree. Supporters see a champion of revolutionary socialism and internationalism; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-pipes">Richard Pipes</a> and Robert Gellately wrote hostile biographies, and Christopher Hill sympathetic ones. Many historians call his government a police state and him a dictator, though Fischer noted that he was not the kind of dictator Stalin became, and Volkogonov wrote that he built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of the party before Stalin's of one man. Western historians long saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stalinism">Stalinism</a> as a continuation of Leninism until revisionists such as Moshe Lewin broke the consensus in the late 1960s. Marxist writers such as Hill and John Rees reject the view that Lenin's government was a dictatorship, and Ryan argues that for Lenin revolutionary violence was a means to a world without violence. In 2024 a Levada poll found 67% of Russians believed he played a positive role in the country's history.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Vladimir Lenin&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy, Within the Soviet Union, In the international communist movement:</p><ul><li>Robert Service, Lenin: A Biography (Macmillan, 2000)</li><li>James Ryan, Lenin's Terror: The Ideological Origins of Early Soviet State Violence (Routledge, 2012)</li><li>Stephen J. Lee, Lenin and Revolutionary Russia (Routledge, 2003)</li><li>Christopher Read, Lenin: A Revolutionary Life (Routledge, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Vladimir Lenin (1870–1924) led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> to power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in October 1917 and headed the Soviet government until his death. His government won the civil war and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, but ruled through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a> and the Red Terror. Historians and political camps still disagree about him.</em></p>

